<h2>The material, audited</h2>
<p>By the time the application was finally heard, the Court had monitored the investigation across three dates — and the audit trail it records is the judgment&rsquo;s backbone:</p>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>May 2026.</strong> On perusing the investigation file, the Court observed that the IO had made no attempt to locate CCTV cameras, although the alleged incident occurred outside a club — a location that generally has them.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>August 2026.</strong> Defence counsel produced four photographs of the spot showing cameras — contradicting the status report&rsquo;s assertion that none existed. The photographs were handed to the IO to retrieve footage. The State also claimed WhatsApp chats showed the accused texting the complainant and expressing remorse; the defence called them forged.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>November 2026.</strong> The IO reported the footage could not be retrieved — erased by passage of time. The accused&rsquo;s seized phone contained no such chats; the complainant declined to hand over hers, calling it non-functional, and the IO neither seized it nor sent it for FSL data retrieval. The complainant, served with notice, did not appear — for the second time.</div>
</div>

<h2>What remained of the case</h2>
<p>Stripped of the uncollected evidence, the Court found the material reduced to the complaint itself — lodged after an unexplained four-day delay — and the complainant&rsquo;s statement under Section 183 BNSS. Against that stood the status report&rsquo;s own paragraphs showing the accused had no connection with the salon through which the job offer was allegedly made, full cooperation with the investigation, and no request for custodial interrogation. On those facts, the Court found no reason to deprive the applicant of liberty.</p>

<h2>The doctrinal frame: investigation quality at the 482 BNSS stage</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Factor</th><th>How it played here</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Delay in FIR</td><td>Four unexplained days between incident and FIR weakened the foundational narrative</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Fair-investigation duty</td><td>The Court expressly noted that a diligent IO could have secured the CCTV footage before erasure — investigative default cannot be held against the accused</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Electronic evidence symmetry</td><td>The accused&rsquo;s phone was seized and came up clean; the complainant&rsquo;s was never seized — an asymmetry the Court declined to ignore</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Custodial interrogation</td><td>None sought — removing the usual justification for arrest in such cases</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Takeaways for practice</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p><strong>Use the status report against its author.</strong> Admissions buried in status reports — no ownership link, no custody request — often decide anticipatory bail.</p>
  <p><strong>Move fast on CCTV.</strong> Footage erases itself. A preservation application at the first hearing, with photographs of camera locations, both secures evidence and exposes investigative lethargy.</p>
  <p><strong>Conditions are the price.</strong> Pre-arrest protection in offence-against-women FIRs almost always carries a no-contact condition and a duty to join the investigation on written notice; breach invites cancellation.</p>
</div>
